To use the Vicuna language model in SwiftUI, you can follow these steps:

1. First, make sure you have imported the CoreML framework into your SwiftUI project.

2. Add the Vicuna language model file to your project. You can find the pre-trained Vicuna language model from sources like Hugging Face or Apple's Model Gallery.

3. Create a class or struct that will handle the predictions using the Vicuna language model. This class should conform to the ObservableObject protocol to update any SwiftUI views when the predictions change.

```swift
import SwiftUI
import CoreML

class VicunaModel: ObservableObject {
    @Published var prediction: String = ""

    func predict(text: String) {
        do {
            let model = try Vicuna(configuration: MLModelConfiguration())
            let input = VicunaInput(text: text)
            let output = try model.prediction(input: input)
            prediction = output.label
        } catch {
            prediction = "Error making prediction"
        }
    }
}
```

4. In your SwiftUI view, create an instance of the VicunaModel class and use it to make predictions with the Vicuna language model.

```swift
import SwiftUI

struct ContentView: View {
    @StateObject var vicunaModel = VicunaModel()
    @State private var inputText = ""

    var body: some View {
        VStack {
            TextField("Enter text", text: $inputText)
                .padding()

            Button("Predict") {
                vicunaModel.predict(text: inputText)
            }

            Text("Prediction: \(vicunaModel.prediction)")
                .padding()
        }
    }
}
```

5. Finally, run your SwiftUI app and test the Vicuna language model predictions by entering different text inputs and pressing the "Predict" button.

This is a basic example of how you can use the Vicuna language model in SwiftUI. You can further customize the user interface and the prediction logic based on your specific requirements.

